{"name":"Cambridge Leather","hex":"#8c633c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"leather","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tough material produced from the skin of animals, by tanning or similar process, used e.g. for clothing; often denotes leather from cattle when no qualifier specifies otherwise.","A piece of the above used for polishing."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of leather.","Referring to one who wears leather clothing (motorcycle jacket, chaps over 501 jeans, boots), especially as a sign of sadomasochistic homosexuality."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leather"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cambridge","blocks":[{"text":"Cambridge is a city and non-metropolitan district in the county of Cambridgeshire, England. It is the county town of Cambridgeshire and is located on the River Cam, 55 miles (89 km) north of London. As of the 2021 United Kingdom census, the population of the City of Cambridge was 145,700; the population of the wider built-up area was 181,137. There is archaeological evidence of settlement in the area dating back to as early as the Bronze Age, and Cambridge became an important trading centre during the Roman and Viking eras. The first town charters were granted in the 12th century, although modern city status was not officially conferred until 1951."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Cambridge_-_Kings_College_vue_des_backs.jpg/330px-Cambridge_-_Kings_College_vue_des_backs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cambridge"},{"title":"Leather","blocks":[{"text":"Leather is a strong, flexible and durable material obtained from the tanning, or chemical treatment, of animal skins and hides to prevent decay. The most common leathers come from cattle, sheep, goats, equine animals, buffalo, pigs and hogs, ostriches, and aquatic animals such as seals and alligators."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Leathertools.jpg/330px-Leathertools.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leather"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Punts%2C_River_Cam_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5936313.jpg/500px-Punts%2C_River_Cam_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5936313.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Punts%2C_River_Cam_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5936313.jpg/500px-Punts%2C_River_Cam_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5936313.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APunts%2C_River_Cam_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5936313.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Church_of_St_Mary_the_Great_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5897670.jpg/500px-Church_of_St_Mary_the_Great_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5897670.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Church_of_St_Mary_the_Great_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5897670.jpg/500px-Church_of_St_Mary_the_Great_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5897670.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChurch_of_St_Mary_the_Great_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5897670.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Trinity_Street%2C_Cambridge_%28geograph_7688455%29.jpg/500px-Trinity_Street%2C_Cambridge_%28geograph_7688455%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Trinity_Street%2C_Cambridge_%28geograph_7688455%29.jpg/1280px-Trinity_Street%2C_Cambridge_%28geograph_7688455%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/